  "final_message": "I will check the git status and explore the codebase.\nI will check the files in `src/`.\nI will check the references to `channel` across the codebase.\nI will check the test files.\nI will wait for `npm test` to finish.\nI will monitor the test execution.\nI am waiting for the build and test process to complete.\nI will wait for the test suite to finish running.\nI will check for any documentation files in the repository root.\nI will check git log.\nI am waiting for git log to complete.\nI will check the git branches and tags.\nI am waiting for the command to finish.\nI will search for any deprecation references in the codebase.\nI am waiting for the search command to complete.\nI will check the initial commit details.\nI am waiting for the git show command to complete.\nI cannot remove the `channel` field from [`OrderPlaced`](file:///C:/avg/w2/r/src/core/events.ts#L7-L26) and the [`Order`](file:///C:/avg/w2/r/src/core/order.ts#L16-L29) model because doing so violates **ARCH-4** in [GEMINI.md](file:///C:/avg/w2/r/GEMINI.md#L27):\n\n> **ARCH-4**: `OrderPlaced` and `OrderCancelled` are consumed by services in other repositories: `billing-service` and `analytics-ingest`. No change may break a deployed consumer: changes are additive; renames and removals go through deprecation.\n\n### Key Considerations\n- **Downstream Consumers**: [`OrderPlaced`](file:///C:/avg/w2/r/src/core/events.ts#L7-L26) is an external contract published to Kafka for services across other repositories (`billing-service` and `analytics-ingest`). Removing `channel` directly is a breaking change.\n- **Domain Model Coupling**: In [`orderPlaced`](file:///C:/avg/w2/r/src/core/events.ts#L42-L59), the event derives its `channel` property directly from [`Order.channel`](file:///C:/avg/w2/r/src/core/order.ts#L23). Removing `channel` from [`Order`](file:///C:/avg/w2/r/src/core/order.ts#L16-L29) would break event generation while the field is still required on the event schema.\n- **Deprecation Cycle Required**: To remove `channel`, it must first undergo a formal deprecation period across consumers rather than an immediate removal.\n",
